{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"LOOP ITERATION 17. Round 10: 6 blocks (up from 1) — but they are DEEPER findings, not regressions, and\none is the most important thing found about set_parent so far. All six verified in source before acting.\n\n1. add_issues_to_epic — REVIEWER RIGHT IN SUBSTANCE, WRONG IN MECHANISM, and the correction matters.\n   The finding cited upstream issue #1244 claiming the method 'unconditionally raises\n   NotImplementedError'. That is NOT true of jira 3.10.5 as installed: the method POSTs to\n   {server}/rest/{agile_rest_path}/{version}/epic/{id}/issue, and the only two NotImplementedError\n   sites in client.py guard group_members and rename_user for Jira < 6.0 — neither is in the Agile\n   path.\n   BUT THE CONCERN IS REAL, and the true mechanism is worse because it fails at RUNTIME rather than\n   at import: AGILE_BASE_REST_PATH defaults to 'agile' (jira/resources.py:1518), while Jira\n   Server/DC serves the Agile API under 'greenhopper'. So with default options the call hits a path\n   that does not exist on DC. The DC client must be built with options={'agile_rest_path':\n   'greenhopper'}, and the spike now tests BOTH paths and records the default's behaviour.\n   This SHARPENS the spike rather than invalidating it — exit criterion (2) was already 'returns 2xx\n   against the harness', which is exactly the test that would have caught it.\n\n2. delete_issue_link — a CALL-SITE caveat I had missed. dispatch_one.py:733 catches\n   subprocess.CalledProcessError and treats it as IDEMPOTENT SUCCESS (_links_applied += 1); the\n   comment says outright 'delete_issue_link shells out via ACLI (raises CalledProcessError, NOT an\n   HTTPError)'. That handler is Cloud/ACLI-specific. DC raises BackendHTTPError (post-J10), which does\n   not match, so a concurrent-removal 404 would ESCAPE and unwind the pass instead of being absorbed.\n   The DC implementation must absorb 404/409 itself, in the transport, rather than relying on a\n   handler written for a different transport.\n\n3. validate_assignee_exists RETURN contract, which I had left unspecified. outbound_assignee.py:108-109\n   does 'acct = client.validate_assignee_exists(...)' then 'return (acct or None, True, False)' — the\n   value flows on AS the resolved assignee identity. Cloud returns an accountId; DC has none, so DC\n   returns the USERNAME, consistent with NameIdentity and the provider/external_id 5f48 mints.\n   Returning an accountId-shaped value or a bare True would corrupt the resolved identity.\n\n4. remove_label — I had written that it 'mirrors the existing add_label'. It does not. add_label uses\n   Issue.add_field_value('labels', label), which has NO removal counterpart; removal goes through\n   Issue.update's 'update' verb. Both are Issue-resource methods but different calls with different\n   payloads. Corrected rather than left as a plausible-sounding falsehood.\n\n5. The observability AC had no test proving the logger FIRES. Now requires a caplog assertion that the\n   record is emitted AND carries the member name and remote id. An observability requirement with no\n   emission test is precisely the 'absence of errors is not evidence' trap the criterion exists to\n   close — I had reproduced the very failure mode I was legislating against.\n\nRound 11 running."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785493224489322001,"uuid":"4efaeadc-8939-408a-ac44-d2e0c77fb56f"}